\begin{note}
Heredocs expand variables just like double quotes \lstinline{"} do,
and a backslash \lstinline{\} disables that expansion for a single \lstinline{$}.
If the delimiter word is quoted (e.g. \lstinline{<<'EOF'} instead of \lstinline{<<EOF}),
then the shell performs no expansion at all and every line is written out literally.
Recall also that sed only treats \lstinline{$} as the end-of-line anchor
when it is the last character of the regex;
everywhere else \lstinline{$} matches a literal dollar sign.
\end{note}

